1
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:04,420
There are thousands

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:06,420
of paintings of Jesus Christ,

3
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:08,500
but this might be the strangest

4
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,860
one ever made,

5
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:11,700
because the longer you look at it,

6
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:13,780
the more disturbing it becomes.

7
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:16,290
Jesus is staring directly at you.

8
00:00:16,330 --> 00:00:18,450
His face looks almost ghost-like,

9
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:20,050
and everyone around him seems

10
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:21,730
terrified of what they are seeing.

11
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:22,930
But the strangest part

12
00:00:22,970 --> 00:00:24,290
is hidden in the corner,

13
00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:25,730
where three mysterious

14
00:00:25,770 --> 00:00:27,090
people are watching him.

15
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:28,530
And these three people shouldn't

16
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:30,210
even exist in this painting,

17
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:31,490
because they were born almost

18
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:34,580
2,000 years after Jesus died.

19
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:39,730
So why did the artist put them there?

20
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:42,130
And what was he actually trying to tell us?

21
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:43,410
To understand that,

22
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:44,850
we first need to look at what's

23
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:46,330
happening in this painting.

24
00:00:46,370 --> 00:00:47,450
At first it looks

25
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:49,290
like a normal religious scene.

26
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:50,890
Jesus is sitting at a table

27
00:00:50,930 --> 00:00:52,250
holding a piece of bread,

28
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:53,850
two men are sitting beside him

29
00:00:53,890 --> 00:00:55,770
and a woman is standing nearby.

30
00:00:55,810 --> 00:00:57,370
But look at their faces:

31
00:00:57,410 --> 00:00:59,630
one man seems completely shocked,

32
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:01,790
while the other is leaning toward Jesus,

33
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:02,990
almost as if he cannot

34
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:04,350
believe what he's seeing.

35
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:06,030
Even the woman standing beside them

36
00:01:06,070 --> 00:01:07,630
has stopped what she was doing.

37
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:09,550
And then there's Jesus himself.

38
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:10,910
Unlike everyone else,

39
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:12,990
his face looks slightly blurred.

40
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:15,230
There's a strange glow surrounding his body,

41
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:16,830
and the sunlight behind him makes

42
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:18,910
him look almost like an apparition.

43
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:21,710
But why would someone paint Jesus like this?

44
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:23,870
Well, this painting is called Christ

45
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:25,710
and the Disciples at Emmaus.

46
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:27,390
It was created by a French artist

47
00:01:27,430 --> 00:01:29,470
named Pascal Dagnan-Bouveret

48
00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:31,520
between 1896 and 1897,

49
00:01:32,070 --> 00:01:33,630
and the scene comes from a very

50
00:01:33,670 --> 00:01:35,390
interesting story in the Bible.

51
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:37,070
According to the Gospel of Luke,

52
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:39,440
a few days after Jesus was crucified,

53
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,800
two of his followers were walking

54
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:42,720
toward a village called Emmaus.

55
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,080
They were heartbroken.

56
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:45,360
Jesus had been killed

57
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:47,040
and they were still trying to understand

58
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,480
everything that had happened.

59
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:49,760
But while they were walking,

60
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:50,880
a stranger approached

61
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:52,430
them and started talking.

62
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:54,030
He asked them what they were discussing,

63
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:56,750
and they began telling him about Jesus, his death,

64
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:58,990
and everything that had happened in Jerusalem.

65
00:01:59,030 --> 00:02:00,670
The stranger listened carefully,

66
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:01,870
and then began explaining

67
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:03,070
the Scriptures to them.

68
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:05,720
They continued walking together for hours.

69
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:07,480
And here's the strange part:

70
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:09,240
neither of the two men recognized

71
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,160
who this stranger actually was.

72
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,160
When they finally reached Emmaus,

73
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:14,440
it was getting late,

74
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,320
so they invited him to stay and have dinner.

75
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:19,240
And while they were sitting at the table,

76
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:21,480
the stranger picked up a piece of bread,

77
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:24,290
blessed it, and broke it.

78
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:28,300
Suddenly both men recognized him.

79
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:29,820
It was Jesus.

80
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:32,300
The same Jesus they had seen crucified

81
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:34,510
was now sitting right in front of them.

82
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:36,110
And according to the Bible,

83
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:37,950
the moment they recognized him,

84
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:39,700
he disappeared.

85
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:44,390
Now imagine witnessing something like that.

86
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:46,390
You spend hours talking to someone

87
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:48,070
without knowing who he is.

88
00:02:48,110 --> 00:02:49,200
Then suddenly,

89
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,040
you realize that the person sitting in front

90
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:53,760
of you is someone you believed was dead,

91
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,010
and before you can even react, he's gone.

92
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,080
That's the moment the artist

93
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:00,280
wanted to capture.

94
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:01,960
And once you know this story,

95
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,730
the painting starts making a lot more sense.

96
00:03:04,770 --> 00:03:07,290
The two disciples aren't simply surprised,

97
00:03:07,330 --> 00:03:08,490
they're witnessing something

98
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:10,490
they never thought was possible.

99
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:12,170
Even the woman standing nearby

100
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:13,850
looks completely frozen.

101
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:15,450
But what's really fascinating

102
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:18,080
is how the artist painted Jesus.

103
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:19,440
If you look behind him,

104
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:21,840
you'll notice that the sun is setting.

105
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:23,920
Normally, when someone sits directly

106
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:25,520
in front of such a bright light,

107
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:27,680
their face becomes dark because the light

108
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:29,440
is coming from behind them.

109
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:32,110
But here, something different is happening.

110
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:34,190
Jesus' face, his hands,

111
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:36,750
and even his white clothes are glowing.

112
00:03:36,790 --> 00:03:38,270
It's almost as if the light

113
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:39,790
isn't coming from the sun,

114
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:42,030
it's coming from Jesus himself.

115
00:03:42,070 --> 00:03:44,190
And that's probably why his face looks

116
00:03:44,230 --> 00:03:46,230
so different from everyone else's.

117
00:03:46,270 --> 00:03:47,350
The artist wanted him

118
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:49,430
to appear almost supernatural,

119
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:50,950
as though he was somewhere between

120
00:03:50,990 --> 00:03:54,080
the physical world and something beyond it.

121
00:03:54,750 --> 00:03:56,310
But now let's return to those three

122
00:03:56,350 --> 00:03:58,710
mysterious people standing in the corner,

123
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:00,390
because this is where the painting

124
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:02,120
becomes even stranger.

125
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,760
If you look carefully, you'll notice a man,

126
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:07,160
a woman, and a young boy watching

127
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:08,840
everything from the shadows.

128
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:10,600
And unlike the other characters,

129
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:11,560
they're wearing clothes

130
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:13,320
from the nineteenth century.

131
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:15,050
So who are they?

132
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:18,140
Well, the man is none other

133
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,660
than the artist himself,

134
00:04:19,700 --> 00:04:20,860
Pascal Dagnan-Bouveret.

135
00:04:20,900 --> 00:04:23,500
The woman is his wife, Anne-Marie,

136
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,940
and the young boy is their son, Jean.

137
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:29,580
Yes, the artist actually painted himself

138
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:31,830
and his entire family witnessing

139
00:04:31,870 --> 00:04:33,430
the resurrection of Jesus.

140
00:04:33,470 --> 00:04:35,350
And we know this because a surviving

141
00:04:35,390 --> 00:04:36,710
study for the painting helps

142
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:38,550
identify these three figures.

143
00:04:38,590 --> 00:04:40,950
But think about how unusual that is.

144
00:04:40,990 --> 00:04:43,430
The events shown in this painting supposedly

145
00:04:43,470 --> 00:04:46,380
happened nearly 2,000 years earlier.

146
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:48,300
Yet the artist decided to place

147
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:50,380
his own family right there,

148
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,300
as if they were physically present when

149
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,020
Jesus revealed himself to his disciples.

150
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:57,900
And naturally, that raises another question:

151
00:04:57,940 --> 00:05:00,430
why would he do something like this?

152
00:05:02,170 --> 00:05:03,490
To understand that,

153
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:04,610
we need to know a little

154
00:05:04,650 --> 00:05:06,370
about the artist himself.

155
00:05:06,410 --> 00:05:09,170
Dagnan-Bouveret was actually one of the most

156
00:05:09,210 --> 00:05:11,650
successful French painters of his time.

157
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:12,930
He was known for creating

158
00:05:12,970 --> 00:05:15,230
incredibly realistic paintings,

159
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:17,150
and he even used photographs to help

160
00:05:17,190 --> 00:05:19,710
him study people and build his scenes.

161
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:20,750
But during the 1890s,

162
00:05:20,790 --> 00:05:23,230
something began to change.

163
00:05:23,270 --> 00:05:25,710
He started moving away from ordinary scenes

164
00:05:25,750 --> 00:05:28,110
of daily life and became increasingly

165
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:30,310
interested in religious paintings.

166
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:32,470
Some historians believe his wife,

167
00:05:32,510 --> 00:05:35,030
Anne-Marie, who was a devoted Catholic,

168
00:05:35,070 --> 00:05:37,030
may have influenced this change.

169
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:38,630
And this is important because it

170
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:40,630
helps us understand why he placed

171
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:42,760
his family inside the painting.

172
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:43,800
He wasn't simply trying

173
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:45,800
to make the artwork unusual,

174
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,160
he was expressing something

175
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,000
about his own faith.

176
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,640
For him, the miracles described in the Bible

177
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:53,160
weren't just events that happened

178
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:54,780
thousands of years ago,

179
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:56,940
they were experiences that could still hold

180
00:05:56,980 --> 00:05:59,580
meaning for people living in his own time.

181
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:01,900
And by painting himself, his wife,

182
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:04,140
and his son standing beside Jesus,

183
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:05,820
he was showing that they too

184
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:07,020
could become witnesses

185
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:09,100
to this moment through their faith.

186
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:11,660
But there's another interesting detail here.

187
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:13,740
When you stand in front of this painting,

188
00:06:13,780 --> 00:06:15,020
you're almost standing

189
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:17,020
alongside the artist's family.

190
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:19,580
They're watching Jesus, and so are you.

191
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:21,100
And because Jesus is staring

192
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:22,820
directly out of the painting,

193
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:24,020
it almost feels as though

194
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:25,860
he's looking at you as well.

195
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:27,300
So instead of simply showing

196
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:28,900
you an event from the Bible,

197
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:30,260
the artist makes you feel

198
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:31,940
like you're part of it.

199
00:06:31,980 --> 00:06:34,180
And when this painting was first exhibited,

200
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:36,630
it attracted enormous attention.

201
00:06:36,670 --> 00:06:37,830
It was eventually purchased

202
00:06:37,870 --> 00:06:39,430
by Henry Clay Frick,

203
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:41,030
one of the richest industrialists

204
00:06:41,070 --> 00:06:43,430
in America and a famous art collector.

205
00:06:43,470 --> 00:06:44,950
Frick owned paintings by some

206
00:06:44,990 --> 00:06:46,790
of the greatest European artists,

207
00:06:46,830 --> 00:06:49,720
including Rembrandt, Vermeer, and Goya,

208
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:51,160
and he wanted this painting

209
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:52,600
in his collection too.

210
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:54,680
At that time, Dagnan-Bouveret

211
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:56,280
was at the height of his career.

212
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:58,520
His paintings were highly valued.

213
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:02,450
He had received major awards, and in 1900,

214
00:07:02,490 --> 00:07:04,370
he became a member of the prestigious

215
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:06,540
Institut de France.

216
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:09,370
But what happened next is almost

217
00:07:09,410 --> 00:07:11,930
as strange as the painting itself,

218
00:07:11,970 --> 00:07:14,330
because despite all his success,

219
00:07:14,370 --> 00:07:16,910
the artist was eventually forgotten.

220
00:07:16,950 --> 00:07:19,070
As the twentieth century progressed,

221
00:07:19,110 --> 00:07:20,510
people became more interested

222
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:22,590
in newer styles of painting.

223
00:07:22,630 --> 00:07:24,750
Artists like Picasso were changing

224
00:07:24,790 --> 00:07:26,350
what art could look like,

225
00:07:26,390 --> 00:07:28,510
and the realistic academic paintings

226
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:30,190
that Dagnan-Bouveret was famous

227
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:32,550
for slowly fell out of fashion.

228
00:07:32,590 --> 00:07:35,080
By the time he died in 1929,

229
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:36,710
the art world had already

230
00:07:36,750 --> 00:07:38,310
changed dramatically,

231
00:07:38,350 --> 00:07:40,150
and over the following decades

232
00:07:40,190 --> 00:07:42,710
his name became far less familiar.

233
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:44,920
Think about the irony of that.

234
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:46,840
Here was a man who painted himself

235
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,640
and his family into a scene from the Bible,

236
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:51,560
almost as if he wanted to preserve

237
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:53,240
their presence forever.

238
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:54,600
But the world eventually

239
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,760
forgot the man who created it.

240
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:58,520
Today, the painting is preserved

241
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:01,370
at the Carnegie Museum of Art in Pittsburgh.

242
00:08:01,410 --> 00:08:02,650
And more than a century

243
00:08:02,690 --> 00:08:04,090
after it was created,

244
00:08:04,130 --> 00:08:05,770
those three figures are still

245
00:08:05,810 --> 00:08:07,210
standing in the shadows,

246
00:08:07,250 --> 00:08:08,570
watching Jesus reveal

247
00:08:08,610 --> 00:08:10,490
himself to his disciples,

248
00:08:10,530 --> 00:08:13,130
while Jesus continues to stare directly

249
00:08:13,170 --> 00:08:15,110
at whoever looks at the painting.

250
00:08:15,150 --> 00:08:16,630
And perhaps that's what makes

251
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:18,790
this artwork so strange.

252
00:08:18,830 --> 00:08:20,550
Because most religious paintings

253
00:08:20,590 --> 00:08:22,230
show us something that supposedly

254
00:08:22,270 --> 00:08:24,390
happened a very long time ago,

255
00:08:24,430 --> 00:08:26,310
but this one makes us feel as though

256
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:28,620
it's happening right in front of us.

257
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:29,900
And the artist didn't just

258
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:31,900
paint himself into that moment.

259
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:35,350
In a way, he also left a place for us.

260
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:37,980
So now that you know the story

261
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:39,180
behind this painting,

262
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:41,820
look at Jesus' face one more time.

263
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:43,340
Does he still look like someone

264
00:08:43,380 --> 00:08:44,920
sitting at a dinner table?

265
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:46,440
Or does it feel like he's

266
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:47,960
looking directly at you?

267
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,880
I'd love to know what you think.

268
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:51,800
Because once you notice everything

269
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,400
hidden inside this painting,

270
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:54,840
it's very difficult to look

271
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:56,950
at it the same way again.

